---
Part VIII, Chapter 12: Testing the Loom CLI
Package: packages/loom/test/cli.test.ts
Language: TypeScript
---

# Testing the Loom CLI

The CLI is an Effect program over the tangler, the configuration and the store of language services, and the surest test of it is the one a user runs: the command itself, as a real process, in a real folder. Each case here makes a temporary workspace, runs `loom` in it, and reads back what the command printed and what it left on disk.

## Running the CLI as a process

Bun runs the CLI's TypeScript source directly, so a case spawns `bun src/main.ts` with the arguments it wants, from whichever directory it needs, and gets back the exit status and both output streams. A case can add environment variables, which is how the language commands are pointed at a store of their own.

A cold spawn loads the whole tangler before it does anything, and that takes a moment. Under the load of the full suite the default five seconds is not always enough, so every case allows thirty.

=>

import { describe, expect, it as it_ } from 'bun:test'
import { effectify } from '@athrio/effect-test'
import { spawnSync } from 'node:child_process'
import {
  existsSync,
  mkdirSync,
  mkdtempSync,
  readFileSync,
  realpathSync,
  rmSync,
  writeFileSync,
} from 'node:fs'
import { tmpdir } from 'node:os'
import { dirname, join, resolve } from 'node:path'
import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url'

const it = effectify(it_)

const cli = resolve(dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)), '..')
const main = resolve(cli, 'src/main.ts')

const loom = (
  args: ReadonlyArray<string>,
  opts: { readonly cwd?: string; readonly env?: Record<string, string> } = {},
) =>
  spawnSync('bun', [main, ...args], {
    cwd: opts.cwd ?? cli,
    env: opts.env ? { ...process.env, ...opts.env } : process.env,
    encoding: 'utf8',
  })

const SPAWN_TIMEOUT = 30_000

## Looms written as lines

The cases feed the CLI looms of their own, and a loom quoted inside this loom's code would be read as part of it. A line that starts with `#` opens a section here, and an anchor is an anchor wherever it stands. So each fixture is written as a list of its lines, every one in quotes and indented, which no line of the book can mistake for its own. `lines` joins them into the text a file holds, ending on a newline as a file does, and `anchor` spells an anchor in two pieces for the same reason.

=>

const lines = (...rows: ReadonlyArray<string>): string => `${rows.join('\n')}\n`

const anchor = (name: string): string => `::${'['}${name}]`

## A workspace to run it in

A workspace is a temporary folder with a `.loom/config.yaml` in it, the configuration the commands read and write. On macOS a temporary folder sits under `/var`, which is a link to `/private/var`, and the child process prints the resolved path. So `workspace` resolves the path the same way before a case compares anything against it.

The language commands also need a store of services. `userStore` makes an empty one a case can point `LOOM_HOME` at, and `installFake` stands a service in it without installing anything: a folder and a `package.json` where the package would be. `loom add` then finds the service already present and skips the package manager, so the cases run offline.

=>

const workspace = (configYaml: string): string => {
  const dir = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-proj-')))
  mkdirSync(join(dir, '.loom'), { recursive: true })
  writeFileSync(join(dir, '.loom', 'config.yaml'), configYaml)
  return dir
}

const configText = (dir: string): string =>
  readFileSync(join(dir, '.loom', 'config.yaml'), 'utf8')

const userStore = (): string =>
  realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-home-')))

const installFake = (home: string, id: string): void => {
  const pkg = join(home, 'services', 'node_modules', '@athrio', `loom-service-${id}`)
  mkdirSync(pkg, { recursive: true })
  writeFileSync(
    join(pkg, 'package.json'),
    `${JSON.stringify({ name: `@athrio/loom-service-${id}`, version: '0.0.0' })}\n`,
  )
}

## Tangling, and asking for help

The first case is the command Loom exists for. A loom with a section that names its file through `{Tangle}` and its package through the frontmatter tangles to `out.ts` beside it, with the greeting it anchors in place. With no arguments at all, the CLI prints its help, and the help names its commands.

=>

const fixture = lines(
  '---',
  'Language: TypeScript',
  'Package: out.ts',
  '---',
  '',
  '# Greeting',
  '',
  '=>',
  '',
  'export const hi = "hello"',
  '',
  '# Out {Tangle}',
  '',
  '=>',
  '',
  anchor('Greeting'),
)

const tempDoc = (): { readonly dir: string; readonly doc: string } => {
  const dir = mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-cli-'))
  const doc = join(dir, 'doc.loom')
  writeFileSync(doc, fixture)
  return { dir, doc }
}

describe('loom cli', () => {
  it('tangles a file through the `tangle` command', () => {
    const { dir, doc } = tempDoc()
    loom(['tangle', doc])
    expect(readFileSync(join(dir, 'out.ts'), 'utf8')).toContain('export const hi = "hello"')
    rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true })
  }, SPAWN_TIMEOUT)

  it('prints help listing its commands given no arguments', () => {
    const { stdout } = loom([])
    expect(stdout).toContain('tangle')
    expect(stdout).toContain('init')
    expect(stdout).toContain('add')
    expect(stdout).toContain('status')
  }, SPAWN_TIMEOUT)
})

## A project's corpus folder

`loom init` asks its questions only of a folder with no configuration yet, and a question needs a terminal to answer it. A workspace that already holds a configuration is asked nothing, so a subprocess reaches the rest of what `init` does.

That rest includes the corpus folder. `init` creates the folder the configuration names, here `chapters`, and a second run leaves it and the loom inside it exactly as they were. A configuration that names no corpus gets a folder called `corpus`.

=>

describe('loom cli — init', () => {
  it('creates the corpus folder the configuration names, and keeps it on a rerun', () => {
    const dir = workspace('corpus: chapters\nlanguages:\n  typescript: {}\nprimary: typescript\n')
    expect(existsSync(join(dir, 'chapters'))).toBe(false)
    loom(['init'], { cwd: dir })
    expect(existsSync(join(dir, 'chapters'))).toBe(true)
    writeFileSync(join(dir, 'chapters', 'hello.loom'), lines('# Hello'))
    loom(['init'], { cwd: dir })
    expect(readFileSync(join(dir, 'chapters', 'hello.loom'), 'utf8')).toBe(lines('# Hello'))
    rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true })
  }, SPAWN_TIMEOUT)

  it('falls back to a folder named corpus when the configuration names none', () => {
    const dir = workspace('languages:\n  typescript: {}\nprimary: typescript\n')
    loom(['init'], { cwd: dir })
    expect(existsSync(join(dir, 'corpus'))).toBe(true)
    rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true })
  }, SPAWN_TIMEOUT)
})

## Languages and their store

`loom status` reports where the store is, the primary language, and which languages are activated. `loom add` records a language in the configuration, and with the fake service already in the store it does so without installing anything. `loom remove` takes the language out of the configuration and its package out of the store, leaving the other languages as they were.

Where the store is depends on `LOOM_HOME`. A case that sets it gets the store it names. A case that leaves it unset gets the workspace's own, under `.loom/services`.

=>

describe('loom cli — languages', () => {
  it('status reports the store, the primary language, and what is activated', () => {
    const home = userStore()
    const dir = workspace('corpus: corpus\nlanguages:\n  typescript: {}\nprimary: typescript\n')
    installFake(home, 'typescript')
    const { stdout } = loom(['status'], { cwd: dir, env: { LOOM_HOME: home } })
    expect(stdout).toContain('typescript')
    expect(stdout).toContain(join(home, 'services'))
    rmSync(home, { recursive: true, force: true })
    rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true })
  }, SPAWN_TIMEOUT)

  it('add records the language and skips install when the service is present', () => {
    const home = userStore()
    const dir = workspace('corpus: corpus\nlanguages: {}\nprimary: typescript\n')
    installFake(home, 'typescript')
    const { status, stdout } = loom(['add', 'typescript'], {
      cwd: dir,
      env: { LOOM_HOME: home },
    })
    expect(status).toBe(0)
    expect(stdout).toContain('activated typescript')
    expect(configText(dir)).toContain('typescript')
    rmSync(home, { recursive: true, force: true })
    rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true })
  }, SPAWN_TIMEOUT)

  it('remove drops the language and deletes its package from the store', () => {
    const home = userStore()
    const dir = workspace(
      'corpus: corpus\nlanguages:\n  typescript: {}\n  python: {}\nprimary: python\n',
    )
    installFake(home, 'typescript')
    loom(['remove', 'typescript'], { cwd: dir, env: { LOOM_HOME: home } })
    const text = configText(dir)
    expect(text).toContain('python')
    expect(text).not.toContain('typescript')
    expect(
      existsSync(join(home, 'services', 'node_modules', '@athrio', 'loom-service-typescript')),
    ).toBe(false)
    rmSync(home, { recursive: true, force: true })
    rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true })
  }, SPAWN_TIMEOUT)

  it('status uses the workspace .loom/services store when LOOM_HOME is unset', () => {
    const dir = workspace('corpus: corpus\nlanguages:\n  typescript: {}\nprimary: typescript\n')
    mkdirSync(
      join(dir, '.loom', 'services', 'node_modules', '@athrio', 'loom-service-typescript'),
      { recursive: true },
    )
    const { stdout } = loom(['status'], { cwd: dir })
    expect(stdout).toContain(join(dir, '.loom', 'services'))
    rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true })
  }, SPAWN_TIMEOUT)
})

## Explicit blocks

An explicit arrow, `~>`, holds its code until a tilde closes it, so a file whose own lines look like Loom's marks tangles whole: a Markdown file with its headings, a script with its comments. These cases run the real `tangle` over a small book in a workspace, the way a project uses it. `explicitWorkspace` sets one up, a book chapter and the chapter under test in its corpus.

The first case tangles a Markdown file whose headings, fence and comment all stay inside the block, while the prose after the block is prose again. The second leaves a block open, which Loom refuses rather than swallowing the rest of the file, and writes nothing. The third makes sure a plain block is untouched by any of this: a line that starts with a tilde is still code there.

=>

const explicitWorkspace = (chapter: string): string => {
  const dir = workspace('corpus: corpus\nlanguages:\n  typescript: {}\nprimary: typescript\n')
  mkdirSync(join(dir, 'corpus'), { recursive: true })
  writeFileSync(join(dir, 'corpus', 'book.loom'), lines('---', 'Part I: Probe', '---', '', '# Probe'))
  writeFileSync(join(dir, 'corpus', '01-chapter.loom'), chapter)
  return dir
}

describe('loom cli — explicit blocks', () => {
  it('tangles a Markdown file whose headings and fences stay inside the block', () => {
    const dir = explicitWorkspace(
      lines(
        '---',
        'Part I, Chapter 1: A skill',
        'Package: ./',
        'Language: Markdown',
        '---',
        '',
        '# A skill',
        '',
        '## The rule',
        '',
        '=>',
        '',
        'Write for the reader.',
        '',
        '## The skill {Tangle}[SKILL.md]',
        '',
        '~>',
        '',
        '---',
        'name: probe',
        '---',
        '',
        '# Probe',
        '',
        '## The rule',
        '',
        anchor('The rule'),
        '',
        '```bash',
        '# a shell comment',
        'ls ~/bin',
        '```',
        '',
        '~',
        '',
        '## After the block',
        '',
        'Prose that follows the closed block is prose again.',
      ),
    )
    const { status, stderr } = loom(['tangle', 'corpus'], { cwd: dir })
    expect(stderr).not.toContain('refusing')
    expect(status).toBe(0)
    const written = readFileSync(join(dir, 'SKILL.md'), 'utf8')
    expect(written).toContain('name: probe')
    expect(written).toContain('# Probe')
    expect(written).toContain('## The rule')
    expect(written).toContain('Write for the reader.')
    expect(written).toContain('# a shell comment')
    expect(written).toContain('ls ~/bin')
    expect(written).not.toContain('Prose that follows')
    rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true })
  }, SPAWN_TIMEOUT)

  it('refuses an explicit block no tilde closes, and writes nothing', () => {
    const dir = explicitWorkspace(
      lines(
        '---',
        'Part I, Chapter 1: Open',
        'Package: ./',
        'Language: Markdown',
        '---',
        '',
        '# Open',
        '',
        '## The file {Tangle}[OPEN.md]',
        '',
        '~>',
        '',
        '# swallowed heading',
      ),
    )
    const { status, stderr } = loom(['tangle', 'corpus'], { cwd: dir })
    expect(status).not.toBe(0)
    expect(stderr).toContain('Unclosed explicit block')
    expect(existsSync(join(dir, 'OPEN.md'))).toBe(false)
    rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true })
  }, SPAWN_TIMEOUT)

  it('keeps a line that starts with a tilde as code in a plain block', () => {
    const dir = explicitWorkspace(
      lines(
        '---',
        'Part I, Chapter 1: Paths',
        'Package: ./',
        'Language: TypeScript',
        '---',
        '',
        '# Paths',
        '',
        '## The paths {Tangle}[paths.ts]',
        '',
        '=>',
        '',
        'export const home =',
        '~/bin',
        'export const done = true',
      ),
    )
    const { status } = loom(['tangle', 'corpus'], { cwd: dir })
    expect(status).toBe(0)
    const written = readFileSync(join(dir, 'paths.ts'), 'utf8')
    expect(written).toContain('~/bin')
    expect(written).toContain('export const done = true')
    rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true })
  }, SPAWN_TIMEOUT)
})

## Orphans past a broken fixture

`loom orphans` tangles every loom the project holds before it lists anything, so a loom that should stand outside the project is the one it trips on. A fixture broken on purpose is the plainest case. These cases build a project with a real orphan and a broken fixture beside it.

`projectWith` makes a workspace with `projectConfig`, a configuration that names a corpus and a primary language, and writes each loom it is given at its path. `fileLoom` gives a loom whose one section tangles a single file to the project's root. `brokenLoom` anchors a section that does not exist, so any tangle that reaches it refuses. `lockOf` reads back the paths the lock records.

`orphanedProject` sets the scene the first cases share. Tangling the whole workspace with `loom tangle .` writes `one.ts` and `two.ts` and passes the broken fixture by. Then the loom that wrote `two.ts` is deleted, which leaves `two.ts` on disk with nothing behind it. `orphans` lists exactly that file. `--prune` deletes it, and leaves the lock holding what the project still produces. When a loom the project holds is broken, `orphans` refuses and prints no path, so a pipe into `rm` deletes nothing.

=>

const projectConfig = 'corpus: corpus\nprimary: typescript\n'

const projectWith = (
  looms: Record<string, string>,
  config: string = projectConfig,
): string => {
  const dir = workspace(config)
  Object.entries(looms).forEach(([path, text]) => {
    mkdirSync(dirname(join(dir, path)), { recursive: true })
    writeFileSync(join(dir, path), text)
  })
  return dir
}

const fileLoom = (file: string): string =>
  lines(`# Writing ${file} {Tangle}[${file}]`, '', '=>', '', `export const file = '${file}'`)

const brokenLoom = lines('# A broken file {Tangle}[broken.ts]', '', '=>', '', anchor('Nowhere'))

const lockOf = (dir: string): ReadonlyArray<string> =>
  JSON.parse(readFileSync(join(dir, '.loom', 'loom.lock'), 'utf8')).tangled

const orphanedProject = (): string => {
  const dir = projectWith({
    'corpus/one.loom': fileLoom('one.ts'),
    'corpus/two.loom': fileLoom('two.ts'),
    'test/broken.fixture.loom': brokenLoom,
  })
  const { status, stderr } = loom(['tangle', '.'], { cwd: dir })
  expect(stderr).not.toContain('refusing')
  expect(status).toBe(0)
  expect(lockOf(dir)).toEqual(['one.ts', 'two.ts'])
  rmSync(join(dir, 'corpus', 'two.loom'))
  return dir
}

describe('loom cli — orphans', () => {
  it('lists the file whose loom is gone, past a broken fixture', () => {
    const dir = orphanedProject()
    const { status, stdout, stderr } = loom(['orphans'], { cwd: dir })
    expect(stderr).not.toContain('refusing')
    expect(status).toBe(0)
    expect(stdout).toBe(`${join(dir, 'two.ts')}\n`)
    rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true })
  }, SPAWN_TIMEOUT)

  it('prunes that file and rewrites the lock to what the project produces', () => {
    const dir = orphanedProject()
    const { status } = loom(['orphans', '--prune'], { cwd: dir })
    expect(status).toBe(0)
    expect(existsSync(join(dir, 'two.ts'))).toBe(false)
    expect(existsSync(join(dir, 'one.ts'))).toBe(true)
    expect(lockOf(dir)).toEqual(['one.ts'])
    rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true })
  }, SPAWN_TIMEOUT)

  it('refuses, and lists nothing, when a loom the project holds is broken', () => {
    const dir = orphanedProject()
    writeFileSync(join(dir, 'corpus', 'broken.loom'), brokenLoom)
    const { status, stdout, stderr } = loom(['orphans'], { cwd: dir })
    expect(status).not.toBe(0)
    expect(stdout).toBe('')
    expect(stderr).toContain('refusing')
    rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true })
  }, SPAWN_TIMEOUT)
})

## Every rule that leaves a loom out

Each remaining rule gets a project of its own: a corpus with one loom that tangles cleanly, and a broken loom placed where the rule under test should leave it out. `orphans` exits cleanly only if that rule held, since any broken loom it reached would make it refuse. `cleanOrphans` runs it and checks exactly that.

Git's rules need a real repository. `git` runs a Git command in a folder, configured so that a person's own Git settings, such as signing every commit, cannot get in the way. The first case ignores one folder through a `.gitignore` and another through the repository's `info/exclude`. The second makes another checkout with `git worktree` and runs `orphans` inside it. That checkout shares the main checkout's `info/exclude` and keeps none of its own.

The configuration's own patterns come next. One case lists a folder under `ignore:`. Another brings fixtures back with a `!` pattern, after which `orphans` reaches the broken fixture and refuses it. Then comes a nested project, a folder holding its own `.loom`, whose looms belong to it rather than to the project around it. Last comes weaving: the woven book leaves out a chapter the project ignores, since weave discovers its corpus by the same rule.

=>

const cleanOrphans = (dir: string): void => {
  const { status, stderr } = loom(['orphans'], { cwd: dir })
  expect(stderr).not.toContain('refusing')
  expect(status).toBe(0)
}

const git = (dir: string, ...args: ReadonlyArray<string>) =>
  spawnSync(
    'git',
    [
      '-c', 'user.name=Loom',
      '-c', 'user.email=loom@example.com',
      '-c', 'commit.gpgsign=false',
      '-c', 'core.hooksPath=/dev/null',
      ...args,
    ],
    { cwd: dir, encoding: 'utf8' },
  )

describe('loom cli — the looms a project leaves out', () => {
  it('leaves out what Git ignores, through .gitignore and info/exclude', () => {
    const dir = projectWith({
      'corpus/one.loom': fileLoom('one.ts'),
      'scratch/broken.loom': brokenLoom,
      'checkouts/copy/broken.loom': brokenLoom,
    })
    git(dir, 'init', '-q')
    writeFileSync(join(dir, '.gitignore'), 'scratch/\n')
    mkdirSync(join(dir, '.git', 'info'), { recursive: true })
    writeFileSync(join(dir, '.git', 'info', 'exclude'), 'checkouts/\n')
    cleanOrphans(dir)
    rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true })
  }, SPAWN_TIMEOUT)

  it('reads the info/exclude a worktree shares with its main checkout', () => {
    const repo = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-repo-')))
    git(repo, 'init', '-q')
    git(repo, 'commit', '-q', '--allow-empty', '-m', 'start')
    mkdirSync(join(repo, '.git', 'info'), { recursive: true })
    writeFileSync(join(repo, '.git', 'info', 'exclude'), 'scratch/\n')
    const checkout = `${repo}-checkout`
    git(repo, 'worktree', 'add', '-q', '--detach', checkout)
    mkdirSync(join(checkout, '.loom'), { recursive: true })
    writeFileSync(join(checkout, '.loom', 'config.yaml'), projectConfig)
    mkdirSync(join(checkout, 'corpus'), { recursive: true })
    writeFileSync(join(checkout, 'corpus', 'one.loom'), fileLoom('one.ts'))
    mkdirSync(join(checkout, 'scratch'), { recursive: true })
    writeFileSync(join(checkout, 'scratch', 'broken.loom'), brokenLoom)
    cleanOrphans(checkout)
    rmSync(checkout, { recursive: true, force: true })
    rmSync(repo, { recursive: true, force: true })
  }, SPAWN_TIMEOUT)

  it('leaves out a folder the configuration lists under ignore', () => {
    const dir = projectWith(
      {
        'corpus/one.loom': fileLoom('one.ts'),
        'drafts/broken.loom': brokenLoom,
      },
      `${projectConfig}ignore:\n  - drafts/\n`,
    )
    cleanOrphans(dir)
    rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true })
  }, SPAWN_TIMEOUT)

  it('brings fixtures back with a ! pattern, and then refuses a broken one', () => {
    const dir = projectWith(
      {
        'corpus/one.loom': fileLoom('one.ts'),
        'test/broken.fixture.loom': brokenLoom,
      },
      `${projectConfig}ignore:\n  - '!*.fixture.loom'\n`,
    )
    const { status, stderr } = loom(['orphans'], { cwd: dir })
    expect(status).not.toBe(0)
    expect(stderr).toContain('broken.fixture.loom')
    rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true })
  }, SPAWN_TIMEOUT)

  it('leaves a nested project to itself', () => {
    const dir = projectWith({
      'corpus/one.loom': fileLoom('one.ts'),
      'inner/.loom/config.yaml': projectConfig,
      'inner/broken.loom': brokenLoom,
    })
    cleanOrphans(dir)
    rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true })
  }, SPAWN_TIMEOUT)

  it('weaves a book without the chapters the project leaves out', () => {
    const dir = projectWith({
      'corpus/book.loom': lines('---', 'Part I: Probe', '---', '', '# Probe'),
      'corpus/01-kept.loom': lines(
        '---', 'Part I, Chapter 1: A kept chapter', 'Language: Prose', '---', '', '# A kept chapter', '', 'Kept.',
      ),
      'corpus/02-draft.fixture.loom': lines(
        '---', 'Part I, Chapter 2: A left-out chapter', 'Language: Prose', '---', '', '# A left-out chapter', '', 'Left out.',
      ),
    })
    const { status } = loom(['weave', 'corpus/book.loom', 'woven.json'], { cwd: dir })
    expect(status).toBe(0)
    const woven = readFileSync(join(dir, 'woven.json'), 'utf8')
    expect(woven).toContain('A kept chapter')
    expect(woven).not.toContain('A left-out chapter')
    rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true })
  }, SPAWN_TIMEOUT)
})

## A loom named directly

A loom the project leaves out still tangles when a person names it, since naming a loom is asking for it. Its files are not the project's, though, so the lock does not take them in, and `orphans` neither lists them nor prunes them.

=>

describe('loom cli — an ignored loom named directly', () => {
  it('tangles it, and keeps its files out of the lock', () => {
    const dir = projectWith({
      'corpus/one.loom': fileLoom('one.ts'),
      'test/sample.fixture.loom': fileLoom('sample.ts'),
    })
    loom(['tangle', 'corpus'], { cwd: dir })
    const { status } = loom(['tangle', 'test/sample.fixture.loom'], { cwd: dir })
    expect(status).toBe(0)
    expect(readFileSync(join(dir, 'test', 'sample.ts'), 'utf8')).toContain("'sample.ts'")
    expect(lockOf(dir)).toEqual(['one.ts'])
    expect(loom(['orphans'], { cwd: dir }).stdout).toBe('')
    rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true })
  }, SPAWN_TIMEOUT)
})

## Setting up Helix

`loom helix` writes into Helix's configuration folder, which it finds through `XDG_CONFIG_HOME`, or `APPDATA` on Windows. A case points both at a temporary folder, so the command writes there and never into the configuration of the person running the tests. `helixHome` makes that folder with a `languages.toml` already in it, holding the lines the case starts from.

The first case runs the command twice over a file that already configures another language. The other language survives, Loom appears exactly once, and the compiled grammar and the queries sit in the runtime folder. The compile needs a C compiler, which every machine that builds Loom already has. The second case starts from a file that sets Loom up by hand: the command refuses, says why, and leaves the file as it was.

=>

const helixHome = (
  ...rows: ReadonlyArray<string>
): { readonly config: string; readonly helix: string; readonly env: Record<string, string> } => {
  const config = realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'loom-helix-')))
  const helix = join(config, 'helix')
  mkdirSync(helix, { recursive: true })
  writeFileSync(join(helix, 'languages.toml'), lines(...rows))
  return { config, helix, env: { XDG_CONFIG_HOME: config, APPDATA: config } }
}

describe('loom cli — helix', () => {
  it('builds the grammar, copies the queries, and adds Loom to languages.toml once', () => {
    const { config, helix, env } = helixHome('[[language]]', 'name = "rust"')
    expect(loom(['helix'], { env }).status).toBe(0)
    expect(loom(['helix'], { env }).status).toBe(0)
    const languages = readFileSync(join(helix, 'languages.toml'), 'utf8')
    expect(languages).toContain('name = "rust"')
    expect(languages.match(/name = "loom"/g)?.length).toBe(1)
    const library = process.platform === 'win32' ? 'loom.dll' : 'loom.so'
    expect(existsSync(join(helix, 'runtime', 'grammars', library))).toBe(true)
    expect(existsSync(join(helix, 'runtime', 'queries', 'loom', 'highlights.scm'))).toBe(true)
    rmSync(config, { recursive: true, force: true })
  }, SPAWN_TIMEOUT)

  it('leaves a languages.toml that sets Loom up by hand as it was', () => {
    const { config, helix, env } = helixHome('[[language]]', 'name = "loom"')
    const { status, stderr } = loom(['helix'], { env })
    expect(status).toBe(1)
    expect(stderr).toContain('already sets up Loom')
    expect(readFileSync(join(helix, 'languages.toml'), 'utf8')).toBe(
      lines('[[language]]', 'name = "loom"'),
    )
    rmSync(config, { recursive: true, force: true })
  }, SPAWN_TIMEOUT)
})

